191
00:06:27,880 --> 00:06:32,600
a lot about why the margins are so thin.

192
00:06:30,200 --> 00:06:34,360
It starts at an oil field. Crude oil is

193
00:06:32,600 --> 00:06:35,880
pumped out of the ground and transported

194
00:06:34,360 --> 00:06:38,520
by pipeline or tanker ship to a

195
00:06:35,880 --> 00:06:40,080
refinery. At the refinery, it goes

196
00:06:38,520 --> 00:06:42,320
through a process that converts raw

197
00:06:40,080 --> 00:06:44,640
crude into finished products, regular

198
00:06:42,320 --> 00:06:46,480
unleaded gasoline, premium gasoline,

199
00:06:44,640 --> 00:06:47,919
diesel fuel. Those finished products

200
00:06:46,480 --> 00:06:49,720
then travel through pipelines to

201
00:06:47,919 --> 00:06:52,040
regional storage terminals, which the

202
00:06:49,720 --> 00:06:54,280
industry simply calls the rack. At the

203
00:06:52,040 --> 00:06:56,440
rack, your fuel is loaded onto a tanker

204
00:06:54,280 --> 00:06:58,240
truck, the big 18-wheeler you see

205
00:06:56,440 --> 00:07:00,960
pulling into stations at night, which

206
00:06:58,240 --> 00:07:03,200
carries roughly 8,000 to 10,000 gallons

207
00:07:00,960 --> 00:07:04,919
per delivery to your underground tanks.

208
00:07:03,200 --> 00:07:07,440
That whole journey has costs at every

209
00:07:04,919 --> 00:07:09,919
step. Crude oil pricing, refining

210
00:07:07,440 --> 00:07:11,720
overhead, pipeline tariffs, tanker truck

211
00:07:09,919 --> 00:07:13,320
labor costs and fuel.

212
00:07:11,720 --> 00:07:15,160
All of it lands on your doorstep before

213
00:07:13,320 --> 00:07:16,600
you sell a single gallon. How you buy

214
00:07:15,160 --> 00:07:19,080
