225
00:07:37,240 --> 00:07:41,000
exchange, you are locked into buying

226
00:07:38,840 --> 00:07:43,440
exclusively from that supplier, often

227
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:45,240
for 10 to 15 years. An unbranded dealer

228
00:07:43,440 --> 00:07:47,280
buys fuel on the open market at the rack

229
00:07:45,240 --> 00:07:49,440
price, which is publicly reported every

230
00:07:47,280 --> 00:07:51,160
day by pricing agencies, and tends to be

231
00:07:49,440 --> 00:07:53,320
lower than the branded price. The

232
00:07:51,160 --> 00:07:55,320
trade-off is volatility. Rack prices

233
00:07:53,320 --> 00:07:57,000
swing with global oil markets, and

234
00:07:55,320 --> 00:07:59,440
during a regional refinery outage or

235
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:01,720
supply disruption, unbranded dealers are

236
00:07:59,440 --> 00:08:03,560
the first to lose access to fuel. The

237
00:08:01,720 --> 00:08:05,720
branded dealers get supplied first.

238
00:08:03,560 --> 00:08:08,040
Neither arrangement is obviously better.

239
00:08:05,720 --> 00:08:09,400
One trades flexibility for stability.

240
00:08:08,040 --> 00:08:11,560
The other trades stability for a

241
00:08:09,400 --> 00:08:13,680
slightly lower cost. Here is the part

242
00:08:11,560 --> 00:08:16,840
